                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+hyperdoctrine"} coherent
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tripos"} tripos
                  %p
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-topos"} (0,1)-topos
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
                      %ul
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+element"} regular element
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"}> frame
                        ,
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
                  %h2#theorems Theorems
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
          %h1#ordinal_numbers Ordinal numbers
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_pages"} Related pages
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"}  References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em ordinal numbers
            (or just
            = succeed ")" do
              %em ordinals
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numbers"} numbers
            of possibly infinite magnitudes. Specifically, ordinal numbers generalise the concept of ‘the next number after …’ or ‘the index of the next item after …’. In particular, the next number after the natural numbers is the first infinite ordinal number.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Naïvely, an
            %strong ordinal number
            should be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            class of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordered+sets"} well-ordered sets
            %strong ordinal rank
            of a well-ordered set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            would be its isomorphism class. That is:
          %ol
            %li every well-ordered set has a unique ordinal number as its ordinal rank;
            %li every ordinal number is the ordinal rank of some well-ordered set;
            %li two well-ordered sets have the same ordinal rank if and only if they are isomorphic as well-ordered sets.
          %p
            Then a
            %strong finite ordinal
            is the ordinal rank of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            %strong infinite ordinal
            or
            %strong transfinite ordinal
            is the ordinal rank of an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+set"} infinite set
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %p
            Taking this definition literally in material
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
            (so one could not make further sets using them as elements). For this reason, in axiomatic set theory one usually defines an ordinal number as a particular representative of this equivalence class. One particularly slick definition is due to von Neumann:
          %ul
            %li
              An
              %strong ordinal
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+set"} transitive
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+set"} pure set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              which is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordered"} well-ordered
              by the membership relation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∈
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
              %strong ordinal rank
              of a well-ordered set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is the unique ordinal number that is isomorphic (as a well-ordered set) to
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            These pure sets are the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong von Neumann ordinals
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"} axiom of foundation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            is automatically a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relation"} transitive relation
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+ = X \cup \{X\}
          %p
            In the appendix of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Kelley"} John Kelley
          %ul
            %li
              An
              %strong ordinal
              is a class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              such that every element of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a subset of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B \in X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \in A
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=B
          %p
            It is later proven that in the axiomatic setting of the appendix (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morse-Kelley+set+theory"}> Morse-Kelley set theory
            ), ordinals are sets.
          %p
            From the perspective of structural
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            to care about distinctions between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
          %ul
            %li
              An
              %strong ordinal
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordered+set"} well-ordered set
          %p
            However, one still may need sets of ordinals, that is sets that serve as the target of an ordinal rank function satisfying (1–3) on any (small) collection of well-ordered sets. One can construct this as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+set"} quotient set
            of that collection.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            The class of ordinals is itself
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordered"} well-ordered
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha\lt\beta
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            is isomorphic to a proper
            %em initial segment
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            (that is, a subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊊
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\subsetneq \beta
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in S
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\lt b
            imply
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in S
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha\in\beta
          %p
            Every ordinal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            has a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/successor"} successor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi α
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha^+
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi α
                      %mo +
                    %mo =
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha^+ = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}
            %strong limit ordinal
            is any ordinal which is not a successor of any other ordinal.
          %p
            In the presence of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
            can be defined as a special ordinal number, specifically an ordinal which is not equipollent (isomorphic as a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+ordinal"}> countable ordinal
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uncountable+ordinal"} uncountable ordinal
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+arithmetic"} ordinal arithmetic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal"} cardinal
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plump+ordinal"} plump ordinal
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-number"} ∞-number
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+analysis"} ordinal analysis
          %p One important use of ordinals is to index transfinite constructions, such as:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+composition"} transfinite composition
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+construction+of+free+algebras"} transfinite construction of free algebras
          %h2#references  References
          %p
            For ordinal numbers in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %em> Homotopy Type Theory: Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
              , The
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Univalent+Foundations+Project"} Univalent Foundations Project
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/book/"} web
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://hottheory.files.wordpress.com/2013/03/hott-online-323-g28e4374.pdf"} pdf
          %p On the equivalence of set-theoretic and type-theoretic ordinals, see:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+de+Jong"}> Tom de Jong
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolai+Kraus"} Nicolai Kraus
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredrik+Nordvall+Forsberg"} Fredrik Nordvall Forsberg
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chuangjie+Xu"} Chuangjie Xu
              %em Ordinals.CumulativeHierarchy.html
              (
              %a{:href => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~mhe/TypeTopology/Ordinals.CumulativeHierarchy.html"}> web
              )
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinals"} ordinals
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/marked+extensional+well-founded+orders"} marked extensional well-founded orders
          %ul
            %li#deJongEtAl23
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+de+Jong"}> Tom de Jong
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolai+Kraus"} Nicolai Kraus
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredrik+Nordvall+Forsberg"} Fredrik Nordvall Forsberg
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chuangjie+Xu"} Chuangjie Xu
              %em Set-Theoretic and Type-Theoretic Ordinals Coincide
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.10696"}> arXiv:2301.10696
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On ordinal exponentiation in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
          %ul
            %li#deJongEtAl25
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+de+Jong"}> Tom de Jong
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolai+Kraus"} Nicolai Kraus
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredrik+Nordvall+Forsberg"} Fredrik Nordvall Forsberg
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chuangjie+Xu"} Chuangjie Xu
              %em Ordinal Exponentiation in Homotopy Type Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.14542"}> arXiv:2501.14542
              )
          %p Basic set-theory including ordinals is developped in the appendix of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Kelley"}> John Kelley
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em General topology
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.springer.com/gp/book/9780387901251"} ISBN:978-0-387-90125-1
        .revisedby
